From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 4 Jul 2026 00:26:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05617/11362] [outer-loop] correct a wrong attribution in the
 TRM-as-ARC-generator note

Operator asked "did we already try that before?" -- a direct challenge to
verify the note's own "never attempted" framing before trusting it.
Investigating properly (not just reasserting the earlier claim) found a
real error in the note itself, not just confirmation.

The note had cited results/trm_arc_baseline_arc_v1.json/arc_v2.json
(pass@1000 = 0.005/0.025) as a "weak sudoku-trained checkpoint tested on
ARC" attempt. Checked the actual eval harness and git history: arc_v1/
arc_v2 are just named checkpoint slots referring to the OFFICIAL arcprize/
trm_arc_prize_verification checkpoint (genuinely ARC-trained, not Sudoku).
The 0.005/0.025 numbers are most likely a methodology artifact -- the same
checkpoint, reproduced properly on 2026-06-09 (results/trm_verifier_
rerank_opportunity.json, commit 5f56ccc37, bounded to complete each task's
full augmentation vote per its own commit message's explicit caveat about
unbounded evals risking incomplete voting) scored pass@2 ~0.52, pass@1000
~0.62 on a 29-task subset -- genuinely strong, matching the checkpoint's
known published range.

Corrected conclusion: static ARC-1 grid-transform solving with TRM has
been tried and genuinely works well -- a STRONGER existing precedent than
originally stated, not a weaker one. This does not change the note's
actual claim (which was never about static grid-transform solving): no
TRM has been trained on or evaluated against ARC-AGI-3's live, interactive
action-sequence format, at any performance level, in any artifact found.
That distinction is what GAP-ARC-TRM-TRAINED-ON-ARC and this note are
actually about, and it holds unchanged after this correction.

Fixed in both docs/research-notes/trm-arc-action-sequence-generator-
2026-07-04.md (the "what's already been tried" section + cross-references)
and ops/known-issues.md (the planner-facing flag entry).

